%[text] # Unicycle Model — Plan-View Vehicle Kinematics
%[text] ## Scenario
%[text] Many vehicles seen from above — a small surface craft, a wheeled robot, an aircraft in level flight — can be approximated by the **unicycle model**. The vehicle has a position $(x, y)$ in the plane and a heading angle $\\theta$ measured from east. It always moves in the direction it points, with forward speed $v$ and turn rate $\\omega$.
%[text] ![](text:image:7bbe)
%[text] (Figure adapted from ROS [Wheeled Mobile Robot Kinematics](https://control.ros.org/iron/doc/ros2_controllers/doc/mobile_robot_kinematics.html).)
%[text] In this assignment you will build the model, simulate it open-loop to confirm the behavior, and then close a feedback loop that steers the vehicle toward a fixed waypoint.
%%
%[text] ## The Kinematic Model
%[text] The state vector has three components:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{c} x \\\\ y \\\\ \\theta \\end{array} \\right\\}$
%[text] The equations of motion are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\dot{x} = v\\cos\\theta\\\\ \\dot{y} = v\\sin\\theta \\\\\\dot{\\theta} = \\omega$
%[text] The velocity vector $\\{\\dot{x}, \\dot{y}\\}$ has magnitude $v$ and direction $\[\\cos\\theta, \\sin\\theta\]$. 
%%
%[text] ## Rate Function (Open Loop)
%[text] Write a local function `unicycle(t, xx, params)` that returns $\\dot{\\mathbf{x}}$ as a column vector — using the state vector convention described above.
%[text] The third argument `params = [v; omega]` lets you change the inputs (forward speed and turn rate) without rewriting the function. These two values represent the motion commands being sent to the vehicle: forward velocity and yaw rate.
%[text] Test it at the initial state $\\mathbf{x}\_0 = \[0;\\ 0;\\ 0\]$ with $v = 2$ m/s and $\\omega = 0$. Heading is east and turn rate is zero, so the result should be $\[2;\\ 0;\\ 0\]$ — moving east at 2 m/s, not turning.
clear

function dxxdt = unicycle(t, xx, params)
    v     = params(1);
    omega = params(2);
    % YOUR CODE
end

xx0 = [0; 0; 0];
rate_check = unicycle(0, xx0, [2; 0]) %[output:7ed0b41e]
%%
%[text] ## Verify with Open-Loop Trajectories
%[text] ### Straight Line
%[text] If we set the inputs (commands) to $v = 2$ m/s and $\\omega = 0$ rad/s, we expect the model solution to be a straight line to the east.  To use `ode45` to solve for this scenario we do the following:
%[text] - Wrap the rate function in an anonymous handle so `ode45` sees the standard `(t, xx)` interface - and with our `param` argument to `unicycle` set to reflect the constant commands; \
v = 2;
omega = 0;
straight = @(t, xx) unicycle(% YOUR CODE);
%[text] - Then, solve with `ode45` over $t \\in \[0, 30\]$ s for the chosen time span; \
tspan = [0, 30];
[t1, X1] = ode45(straight, tspan, xx0);
%[text] - Then plot the results (the unicycle position on an x/y graph) and verify that the trajectory is consistent with the expected straight line for this simple verification case. \
figure(1) %[output:182c7115]
clf() %[output:182c7115]
% YOUR CODE %[output:182c7115]
axis equal %[output:182c7115]
grid on %[output:182c7115]
xlabel('East (m)') %[output:182c7115]
ylabel('North (m)') %[output:182c7115]
title(sprintf('Unicycle, v = %.2f omega = %.2f', v, omega)); %[output:182c7115]
%[text] ### **Circle**
%[text] Another verification test is to set $v = 2$ and $\\omega = 0.5$.  Then verify that the trajectory radius is  $R = v / \\omega = 4 m$. With these values $R = 4$ m. 
%[text] Verify this test case as well using the same process.
v = 2;
omega = 0.5;
circle = @(t, xx) unicycle(t, xx, [v; omega]);
[t2, X2] = ode45(circle, tspan, xx0);
figure(2) %[output:8107459b]
clf() %[output:8107459b]
% YOUR CODE %[output:8107459b]
axis equal %[output:8107459b]
grid on %[output:8107459b]
xlabel('East (m)') %[output:8107459b]
ylabel('North (m)') %[output:8107459b]
title(sprintf('Unicycle, v = %.2f omega = %.2f, Expected R = %.2f', v, omega, v/omega)); %[output:8107459b]
%%
%[text] ## Proportional Guidance - Steer Toward a Waypoint
%[text] The verification step gives us confidence that the MATLAB code is consistent with the equations of motion. We can extend the model to simulate waypoint navigation.
%[text] ![](text:image:39c0)
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text] A simple, and often effective, method is proportional guidance which applies an angular velocity command ($\\omega$) in the direction of the waypoint relative to the vehicle:
%[text] Steering law — turn rate proportional to bearing error:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\omega = K\_p \\, \\theta\_e$
%[text] where $K\_p$ is the proportional gain — a user-tunable parameter that sets how aggressively the vehicle turns toward the goal. 
%[text] **Implementation of bearing error**
%[text] In this case, the mathematical model is simple, but the implementation has to deal with the detail that angles have a finite range, e.g., 0-360 deg or -180--180 deg.  
%[text] Bearing to goal:  Angle from the vehicle to the goal, measured from east:
%[text] $\\theta\_g = \\mathrm{atan2}(y\_g - y,\\; x\_g - x)$
%[text] (Use `atan2`, not `atan` , because `atan2` returns the correct quadrant over the full $\[-\\pi, \\pi\]$.)
%[text] Bearing error:  How far the current heading is from the bearing to the goal. Angles wrap at $\\pm\\pi$, so a naive subtraction can produce a value outside $\[-\\pi, \\pi\]$. There are various ways of doing this (e.g a `while` statement), but a trick is to use `atan2(sin(d), cos(d))` which wraps any angle $d$ back into the correct interval.
%[text] $\\theta\_e = \\mathrm{atan2}\\bigl(\\sin(\\theta\_g - \\theta),\\; \\cos(\\theta\_g - \\theta)\\bigr)$
%[text] ## Closed Loop Equations of Motion
%[text] Substituting the steering law into the open-loop kinematics gives the closed-loop equations of motion.
%[text] $\\dot{x} = v\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\dot{y} = v\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\dot{\\theta} = K\_p \\, \\theta\_e(x, y, \\theta)$
%[text] Note that  $\\dot\\theta$ is now a function of the state .  This is the feedback that closes the loop.
%[text] ### MATLAB Implementation
%[text] We will simluate this scenario where the vehicle starts at the origin and drives to a fixed waypoint.
%[text] Write a new rate function `unicycle_goal(t, xx, Kp)` that implements these equations with 
%[text] - fixed waypoint goal:  $(x\_g,\\, y\_g) = (20,\\, 15)$ m
%[text] - fixed forward speed: $v = 2$ m/s 
%[text] - fixed proportional gain: $K\_p = 1.5$ 1/2 \
function dxxdt = unicycle_goal(t, xx)
    x_g = 20;
    y_g = 15;
    v   = 2;
    Kp = 1.5;
    % YOUR CODE
end
%[text] Simulate (sovle the ODEs) over $t \\in \[0, 25\]$ s.
[t3, X3] = ode45(@unicycle_goal, [0, 25], xx0);
%[text] Visualize (plot) the trajectory
figure() %[output:014c4145]
clf() %[output:014c4145]
plot(X3(:,1), X3(:,2), 'b-', 'LineWidth', 1.5) %[output:014c4145]
hold on %[output:014c4145]
plot(20, 15, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r') %[output:014c4145]
axis equal %[output:014c4145]
grid on %[output:014c4145]
xlabel('East (m)') %[output:014c4145]
ylabel('North (m)') %[output:014c4145]
legend('trajectory', 'goal', 'Location', 'best') %[output:014c4145]
title("Proportional Guidance") %[output:014c4145]
hold off %[output:014c4145]
%[text] You should see the vehicle curve smoothly toward $(20, 15)$ and then circle around it.  The guidance has no stop condition, so the model predicts the vessel will circle the target forever - a limit cycle.
%[text] - Try $K\_p = 0.3$ and $K\_p = 5$.   Do the changes make intuitive sense? \
%[text] ## Verification Test
%[text] The "**Implementation of bearing error"** discussed above is a common source of errors.   Modify the code above to verify that the simulation works for waypoints in each of the four quadrants.   There is nothing additional to write, but this is a step to verify the implementation.
